emulate link() and linkat() correctly under the app seccomp filter

dpkg rotates its status database by hard-linking the current file to
/var/lib/dpkg/status-old before writing a new one, so the Ubuntu E2E run
35035691404 died on

    dpkg: error: error creating new backup file
    '/var/lib/dpkg/status-old': Permission denied

and apt-get install exited 100 with the rootfs's dpkg database left
untouched (no status-old at all, status unmodified).

The call arrives in PRoot as linkat(2), not link(2): glibc implements
link(3) as linkat(AT_FDCWD, ...).  linkat(2) *is* on the app policy's
allowlist (it is in bionic's SYSCALLS.TXT and not in
SECCOMP_BLOCKLIST_APP.TXT, whereas plain link(2) has no entry at all and
is trapped), so unlike PR_link it reaches PRoot's ordinary enter stage
rather than the SIGSYS handler.  There the paths were translated and the
kernel was left to run the call for real, in the tracee's app-domain
process, where the platform refuses hard links in app data - EACCES, from
SELinux's app-data rules and/or from the storage layer, which PRoot
cannot tell apart and does not need to.  The guest saw that bare EACCES as
the "Permission denied" above.  Translating the paths cannot help; the
operation has to be performed by PRoot itself.

The first version of this patch read the failure as a double SIGSYS
(link() rewritten to linkat() and restarted, the restarted linkat trapping
too) and fixed the wrong thing: under the real policy the restarted
linkat is *allowed*, so it ran for real and produced the same EACCES while
the emulation read its arguments from a register layout that no longer
matched the call.  Two facts settle the design:

  - The fork never installs a seccomp filter of its own.
    tracee/event.c's launch_process() replaced the
    enable_syscall_filtering(tracee) call with a no-op, and the function
    has no caller left anywhere, so nothing PRoot adds to
    syscall/seccomp.c's filter can ever be reached.  (The filter a
    tracee runs under is the platform's, inherited from zygote.)
  - A SECCOMP_RET_TRAPped syscall kills the process that makes it even
    with SIG_IGN installed, and with a no-op handler the syscall returns
    the syscall number as its result (measured on this host).  So PRoot's
    own process must only ever call syscalls the app policy allows, and a
    trapped one is a loud death, not something to be caught.

The fix, then, is to do the link in PRoot's own process in whichever stage
the call actually arrives:

  - syscall/enter.c handles PR_linkat and PR_link by translating both
    paths, calling emulate_link(), and - on success - voiding the syscall
    (set_sysnum(PR_void)) and poking SYSARG_RESULT with 0, so the exit
    stage hands the guest 0 and the kernel never sees the call.  On
    failure it returns -errno, which syscall.c turns into the guest's
    error.  PR_linkat keeps linkat semantics (AT_EMPTY_PATH is left to the
    kernel; AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW decides whether the source's final symlink
    is resolved) and PR_link keeps link(2)'s (no flags, never follows a
    symbolic source).
  - tracee/seccomp.c's PR_link and PR_linkat SIGSYS cases call the same
    emulate_link(), so a platform that *does* trap them behaves exactly
    like one that does not.
  - emulate_link() tries the real linkat() first: where the platform
    permits hard links the guest gets one, with the shared inode, and
    nothing is copied.  Only when the platform refuses with EACCES, EPERM,
    EXDEV, ENOSYS or EOPNOTSUPP does it emulate - a symbolic source is
    recreated with symlinkat(), anything else is copied byte for byte
    through an O_CREAT|O_EXCL target (so an existing target still yields
    link(2)'s EEXIST) and the partial target is unlinked if the copy
    fails.  Every other errno (EEXIST, ENOENT, EISDIR, ...) is the
    guest's answer and is returned untouched.
  - Every syscall in emulate_link() runs in PRoot's own process, under the
    same app policy, so only linkat/readlinkat/symlinkat/unlinkat/openat/
    fstat/read/write/close appear there - all allowlisted.  unlinkat(2),
    not unlink(2); link(2) and unlink(2) have no allowlist entry and would
    kill PRoot outright.
  - This also fixes the two defects of that first version: it read
    olddirfd from ORIGINAL (garbage for a direct guest linkat), and its
    fallback called renameat() and then unlink()ed the source - it *moved*
    the file, so every emulated link destroyed its source and dpkg would
    have lost /var/lib/dpkg/status itself.
  - link_diag() lines (to PROOT_SIGSYS_LOG, see 0002) name what the
    emulation decided and why.  This is the one place PRoot invents the
    errno the guest sees, and "Permission denied" with no indication of
    which step produced it is what made this take a device run to find.

Validated on x86_64 Linux against a jammy rootfs, with proot -i 0:0, in
three modes - A: no interference (the kernel allows hard links); B: the
tracer's linkat() forced to EACCES, which is the device's refusal; C: a
harness that installs a filter trapping __NR_link and allowing everything
else, i.e. the app policy exactly.  In all three, dpkg --configure -a
exits 0 and creates status-old (A and C as a true hard link sharing
status's inode, B as a byte copy of it), apt-get install --reinstall -y
hello exits 0 and the installed hello runs.  Under the trap harness the
guest's glibc ln, a raw trapped link(2) and a raw linkat(2) all produce
genuinely hard-linked files with identical inodes, and EEXIST/ENOENT/
ENOTDIR still come from the kernel.  In mode B a symbolic source is
recreated as a symlink rather than followed.

Not upstreamed: no write access to oonid/pr; applied by fetchLinuxSource
in linux/build.gradle.kts (stacks on 0001 and 0002).
